export const OPSCR_SKILL: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  "SKILL.md":
    '---\nname: opscr-architect\ndescription: Design a software architecture with the user through a short interview, then write it as validated opscr manifests (*.opscr.yaml) — applications, API gateways, databases, caches, queues, topics, storage, channels, business capabilities and the relationships between them. Use when the user wants to model, document, sketch or diagram a system\'s architecture as code, describes a system and asks for its manifests, or invokes /opscr-architect.\n---\n\n# opscr-architect\n\nYou turn a conversation about a system into **opscr manifests**: Kubernetes-style YAML documents (`apiVersion` / `kind` / `metadata` / `spec`) that the `opscr` CLI validates against JSON schemas and structural policies. The output must compile with **zero errors**. Never call the result valid unless `opscr compile` said so.\n\nTalk to the user in their language. Free-text values (`description`, `displayName`) follow the user\'s language; keys and enum values are always written exactly as documented.\n\n<!-- Generated by opscr 0.1.0 from schemas/. Do not edit: run `opscr skills install` to regenerate. -->\n\n## 0. Preflight\n\nRun `opscr --version`.\n\n- Works → continue.\n- Not found → tell the user validation needs the CLI (`npm install -g opscr`, or `npm link` from the opscr repo). Offer to continue without it; if they accept, every report must say **"not validated"**.\n\n## 1. Interview — at most 8 questions\n\nGoal: enough to draw the system, not a requirements document.\n\n- Ask **one question at a time**, each with **your recommended answer**, so the user can just say "yes".\n- Skip anything the user already told you. If the first message already covers the topics below, go straight to step 2.\n- Stop as soon as the next topics would not change which manifests or edges exist.\n\nTopics, in order of value:\n\n1. **The solution** — its name and what it is responsible for. This becomes the `ApplicationService` (bounded context) that contains the applications.\n   **Domains** — if the user talks about domains, business areas or several bounded contexts: which `Domain`s, their `Subdomain`s (core / supporting / generic) and which `ApplicationService` implements each. Read `references/patterns/ddd.md` first. Otherwise do not model domains.\n2. **Entry points** — who uses it (`Channel`: mobile, web…) and through what (`APIGateway`, `LoadBalancer`). Only if the user mentions them: your recommended answer is "none modeled" unless the description names a UI, an app, a public API or partners calling in.\n3. **Applications** — what runs your code, in which language, on which runtime (`provider`, e.g. `EKS`, `Lambda`, `Cloud Run`, `OpenShift`).\n4. **State** — durable records (`Database`), disposable data (`Cache`), files and blobs (`Storage`).\n5. **Async flows** — work for one consumer (`Queue`) vs messages fanned out to many subscribers (`Topic`, with its `type`).\n   **Outbound messages** to people — email, SMS, push (`Notification`).\n   **External systems** the solution calls or is called by — payment acquirer, CRM, ERP, partner APIs (`ExternalSystem`). Every one the user mentions gets a manifest and an edge; "updates the CRM" without a CRM in the diagram loses the dependency.\n6. **Platform** — one of `aws`, `azure`, `gcp`, `onpremise`, `self-management`, `saas`. It decides which `provider` values you use (see `references/providers.md`). Default to one platform for everything unless told otherwise.\n7. **Business layer** (only if the user brings it up) — `BusinessCapability`, `BusinessService`, `ServiceOffer`, `BusinessRule`; owners (`Squad`, `Community`).\n\nAnything you assume instead of asking is a **default**; list defaults in the final report.\n\nModel what the user described, not what systems like it usually have. Each manifest should trace back to something the user said; a gateway, a channel, a cache or a business rule "every system has" is noise in the diagram unless the user asks for it.\n\nWhen you do add something the user did not mention — because the design needs it (a dead-letter queue, the database behind a service the user named) or as a default — **mark it as assumed** (see step 3). Assuming is fine; hiding it is not.\n\n## 2. Plan — one confirmation\n\nShow a compact plan and ask once: "Shall I write it?"\n\nMark assumed components with `(assumed)`, and list the files you will write.\n\n```\nDomain: commerce › Subdomain: ordering (core) › ApplicationService: orders\n  Applications: orders-api (Java, EKS) · orders-worker (Java, EKS)\nEntry:      Channel web-app → APIGateway public-api (External)\nState:      Database orders-db (AuroraPostgres) · Cache orders-cache (ElastiCache Redis)\nAsync:      Topic order-events (MSK, DomainEvent)\nEdges:      public-api → orders-api (calls) · orders-api → orders-db (writes) · …\n```\n\n## 3. Write\n\n1. Before writing a Kind, **read its reference** in `references/kinds/` (table below). Use only the fields listed there — unknown fields are errors.\n2. `provider` is a **string naming the concrete product** (`RDSPostgres`, `SQS`, `Cloud Run`), never an object and never just `aws`. Take it from `references/providers.md`.\n3. Read `references/relationships.md` before writing edges.\n4. **Layout** — one directory per solution; components and relationships in separate files (see "File layout" below).\n5. `references/example/` is a complete, valid two-domain solution laid out that way — mirror its style.\n6. **Patterns.** For each pattern in the table at the end whose "Read when" applies, read its guide **before writing** and follow it. Then enable its checks: create `<solution>/opscr.config.yaml` with `packs: [<id>, …]`. Apply patterns the solution actually uses — don\'t add Topics or Queues just to satisfy one.\n\n**File layout.** All files go flat in `<solution>/`; `opscr compile <solution>/` reads every `*.opscr.yaml` in it. Split by **DDD `Domain`** (not by ApplicationService):\n\n| `Domain` manifests | Files |\n|---|---|\n| none or one | `<solution>.opscr.yaml` (all components) + `relationships.opscr.yaml` |\n| two or more | `<domain>.opscr.yaml` per `Domain` (named after it) + `shared.opscr.yaml` + `relationships.opscr.yaml` |\n\n- A domain file holds the `Domain`, its `Subdomain`s, the `ApplicationService`s that belong to them, their `Application`s and the data and messaging only that domain uses. Something used by several domains (channels, a shared gateway) goes in `shared.opscr.yaml`. Placement is about ownership — it does not add `belongsTo` edges.\n- Inside a component file: `Domain` and `Subdomain`s first, then `ApplicationService`s, entry points, `Application`s, data and messaging, business and organization kinds.\n- `relationships.opscr.yaml` holds **only** `Relationship` manifests — one per domain plus one for the entry paths and cross-domain edges is easiest to read.\n- Write the component files first and `relationships.opscr.yaml` last. Until it exists, every component is reported as an orphan — expected, not something to fix.\n- `opscr.config.yaml` sits next to them.\n\n**Assumptions.** Every manifest whose component the user did not mention gets this annotation — the manifest kinds that structure the file (`ApplicationService`, `Relationship`) excluded:\n\n```yaml\nmetadata:\n  name: orders-cache\n  annotations:\n    opscr.dev/assumed: "true"    # quoted: annotation values are strings\n```\n\n`opscr compile` lists each assumed manifest as `info`. Remove the annotation only when the user confirms that component by name.\n\nContainment: every `Application` gets a `belongsTo` edge to its `ApplicationService` — the edge is the only way to say which context an application belongs to. Databases, queues, topics, caches, notifications and gateways are shared infrastructure — no `belongsTo` unless the user says they are owned by the solution.\n\nRules the validator enforces:\n\n- `apiVersion` is one of: `opscr.dev/v1`.\n- `metadata.name`: lowercase, starts with a letter, `[a-z0-9_-]` only; unique per Kind.\n- Every component appears in at least one edge (otherwise: orphan warning). `Squad` and `Community` are exempt.\n- An edge\'s `from` / `to` use the exact `kind` and `metadata.name` of a declared manifest.\n- Edge `type` is one of: `calls`, `consumes`, `produces`, `reads`, `writes`, `subscribes`, `publishes`, `triggers`, `appliesTo`, `belongsTo`, `relatedTo`, and the connection must make sense — `references/relationships.md` lists the ones that never compile (a database calling, anything flowing into a Channel, `reads` on a Queue…).\n- A `BusinessRule`\'s `appliesTo` entries name existing manifests by `kind` + `name`.\n- No cycles unless the user confirms the cycle is real (reported as a warning).\n- No secrets, passwords or tokens in any field.\n\n## 4. Validate — loop until clean\n\n```bash\nopscr compile <solution-dir> --output json\n```\n\nEach diagnostic has `severity`, `message`, `line`, `fieldPath`, `ruleId`, `suggestion`. If a validation hook is installed, the same diagnostics appear right after you write the file.\n\n- Fix **every error**, using `fieldPath` and `line` to find it. Re-read the Kind reference rather than guessing a field or value.\n- Fix warnings too: an orphan gets the edge it is missing; a cycle is either removed or confirmed with the user. Pattern warnings (`eda/…`) are design problems — fix them the way the pattern guide says.\n- `info` diagnostics are recommendations: apply them when they fit, otherwise list them in the report. `assumptions/assumed` is expected — it is how assumed components stay visible.\n- Re-run after each round. After 5 rounds that still fail, stop and show the remaining diagnostics to the user.\n- Finish with `opscr compile <solution-dir>` (human output) so the user sees the result.\n\n## 5. Report\n\n- Files written, and which domain each one holds.\n- Manifests by Kind and number of edges.\n- Assumed components (the `assumptions/assumed` lines of `opscr compile`) and other defaults you chose without asking.\n- Any warning left on purpose, and why.\n\n## Kinds\n\n| Kind | Use for | Reference |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| `APIGateway` | Managed entry point for APIs (auth, throttling, routing by path/version). Channels and partners call it; it calls Applications. Use LoadBalancer instead for plain traffic distribution. | [references/kinds/apigateway.md](references/kinds/apigateway.md) |\n| `LoadBalancer` | Distributes traffic across instances of Applications (ALB/NLB, NGINX, HAProxy, F5). Model it only when it matters to the reader; edges go LoadBalancer → Application with type calls. | [references/kinds/loadbalancer.md](references/kinds/loadbalancer.md) |\n| `Application` | Anything that runs your code — API, worker, BFF, consumer, scheduled job, function. If you write and deploy it, it is an Application. | [references/kinds/application.md](references/kinds/application.md) |\n| `ApplicationService` | A bounded context — the boundary that groups the Applications built for one model of the business (Application → ApplicationService, belongsTo). With a DDD model, it belongs to the Subdomain it implements. Diagrams draw it as the box around its applications. | [references/kinds/applicationservice.md](references/kinds/applicationservice.md) |\n| `Topic` | Publish/subscribe — every subscriber receives each message (SNS, Kafka, Pub/Sub, Event Hubs). Classify it with type. | [references/kinds/topic.md](references/kinds/topic.md) |\n| `Queue` | Point-to-point async work — each message is handled by one consumer. Use Topic when several independent subscribers receive the same message. | [references/kinds/queue.md](references/kinds/queue.md) |\n| `Database` | System of record — data that must survive restarts and is queried (SQL, document, key-value, graph, search). Use Cache when the data can be rebuilt, Storage for files. | [references/kinds/database.md](references/kinds/database.md) |\n| `Cache` | Disposable, fast data in front of something slower (sessions, computed results, rate limits). Losing it costs latency, not data. | [references/kinds/cache.md](references/kinds/cache.md) |\n| `Storage` | Files and blobs addressed by key or path — uploads, exports, reports, backups, static assets. Use Database for queried records. | [references/kinds/storage.md](references/kinds/storage.md) |\n| `Channel` | The front end people interact with. It calls an APIGateway, LoadBalancer or Application; nothing calls it. | [references/kinds/channel.md](references/kinds/channel.md) |\n| `Notification` | A service that delivers messages to people (SES, FCM, Twilio, SendGrid). Applications call it. Not the app the user opens — that is a Channel. | [references/kinds/notification.md](references/kinds/notification.md) |\n| `ExternalSystem` | Anything the solution talks to but does not build or operate — payment acquirer, CRM, ERP, identity provider, partner or government API. Applications call it (or it calls an APIGateway). Do not use it for managed infrastructure that has its own Kind (database, queue, notification service). | [references/kinds/externalsystem.md](references/kinds/externalsystem.md) |\n| `Domain` | A business area in the Domain-Driven Design sense. Model it when the user talks about domains or business areas, or when the solution spans more than one. Subdomains belong to it; it belongs to nothing. Not a technical component — it never takes part in runtime flow. | [references/kinds/domain.md](references/kinds/domain.md) |\n| `Subdomain` | A part of a Domain (Subdomain → Domain, belongsTo). ApplicationServices — the bounded contexts — belong to the subdomain they implement. Classify it with type; it decides where to build versus buy. | [references/kinds/subdomain.md](references/kinds/subdomain.md) |\n| `BusinessCapability` | What the business is able to do (e.g. "Payments", "Customer onboarding"). Annotation, not topology: link it with an appliesTo or relatedTo edge. | [references/kinds/businesscapability.md](references/kinds/businesscapability.md) |\n| `BusinessService` | A service the business delivers to customers or other areas. Annotation, not topology. | [references/kinds/businessservice.md](references/kinds/businessservice.md) |\n| `ServiceOffer` | A commercial offer built on business services (a product the customer buys). Annotation, not topology. | [references/kinds/serviceoffer.md](references/kinds/serviceoffer.md) |\n| `BusinessRule` | A rule the solution must enforce, with explicit constraints. Points at what it governs via appliesTo; also add an appliesTo edge so diagrams can anchor it. | [references/kinds/businessrule.md](references/kinds/businessrule.md) |\n| `Squad` | Team that owns applications. Organization metadata — not drawn as part of the architecture; edges are optional. | [references/kinds/squad.md](references/kinds/squad.md) |\n| `Community` | Group of squads. Organization metadata — not drawn as part of the architecture; edges are optional. | [references/kinds/community.md](references/kinds/community.md) |\n| `Relationship` | The graph. Each edge connects two declared manifests by kind + metadata.name. One Relationship per solution is typical. | [references/kinds/relationship.md](references/kinds/relationship.md) |\n\n## Patterns\n\n| Pack | Pattern | Read when | Guide |\n|------|---------|-----------|-------|\n| `eda` | Event-driven architecture | The solution has a Topic or a Queue, or the user mentions events, commands, async processing, pub/sub or sagas. | [references/patterns/eda.md](references/patterns/eda.md) |\n| `topology` | Topology | Always useful once the solution has more than a couple of components — especially with gateways, load balancers, channels, database triggers or more than one cloud. | [references/patterns/topology.md](references/patterns/topology.md) |\n| `ddd` | Domain-Driven Design | The user talks about domains, subdomains, bounded contexts or business areas, or the solution has more than one ApplicationService. | [references/patterns/ddd.md](references/patterns/ddd.md) |\n',
  "references/kinds/apigateway.md":
    "# APIGateway\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: APIGateway`\n\nAPI Gateway\n\n## When to use\n\nManaged entry point for APIs (auth, throttling, routing by path/version). Channels and partners call it; it calls Applications. Use LoadBalancer instead for plain traffic distribution.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `AWS API Gateway`<br>azure: `Azure API Management`<br>gcp: `Google API Gateway`, `Apigee`<br>self-management: `Kong`, `Tyk`, `KrakenD` |  |\n| `exposure` | string | **yes** | `Internal` · `External` · `InternalExternal` | Who can reach it — Internal (inside the organization), External (internet/partners) or both |\n| `description` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: APIGateway\nmetadata:\n  name: example-apigateway\nspec:\n  provider: AWS API Gateway\n  exposure: Internal\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/application.md":
    "# Application\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Application`\n\nTechnical or business application\n\n## When to use\n\nAnything that runs your code — API, worker, BFF, consumer, scheduled job, function. If you write and deploy it, it is an Application.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `ECS`, `EKS`, `Lambda`, `Fargate`, `EC2`, `App Runner`<br>azure: `AKS`, `Azure Functions`, `Azure App Service`, `Azure Container Apps`<br>gcp: `GKE`, `Cloud Run`, `Cloud Functions`, `App Engine`<br>onpremise: `OpenShift`, `VM`<br>self-management: `Kubernetes`, `Docker` | Runtime platform the application is deployed on |\n| `language` | string | **yes** | `Java` · `Python` · `Go` · `TypeScript` · `JavaScript` · `Kotlin` · `CSharp` · `Rust` · `Ruby` · `PHP` · `Scala` · `Elixir` |  |\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  |  |\n| `interfaces` | array of object |  |  | Network interfaces exposed by this application |\n| `interfaces[].port` | integer |  | 1–65535 | Port number |\n| `interfaces[].protocol` | string |  | `http` · `https` · `tcp` · `grpc` · `ws` · `wss` |  |\n| `interfaces[].path` | string |  |  | URL path prefix (for http/https) |\n| `interfaces[].host` | string |  |  | Override host (defaults to service name) |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: example-application\nspec:\n  provider: ECS\n  language: Java\n  description: example-description\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/applicationservice.md":
    "# ApplicationService\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: ApplicationService`\n\nApplication service — a boundary that groups the applications delivering one capability\n\n## When to use\n\nA bounded context — the boundary that groups the Applications built for one model of the business (Application → ApplicationService, belongsTo). With a DDD model, it belongs to the Subdomain it implements. Diagrams draw it as the box around its applications.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  | What this bounded context is responsible for |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  | Friendly display name for the application service |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: ApplicationService\nmetadata:\n  name: example-applicationservice\nspec:\n  description: example-description\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/businesscapability.md":
    '# BusinessCapability\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: BusinessCapability`\n\nBusiness capability\n\n## When to use\n\nWhat the business is able to do (e.g. "Payments", "Customer onboarding"). Annotation, not topology: link it with an appliesTo or relatedTo edge.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  |  |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  | Friendly display name |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: BusinessCapability\nmetadata:\n  name: example-businesscapability\nspec:\n  description: example-description\n```\n',
  "references/kinds/businessrule.md":
    "# BusinessRule\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: BusinessRule`\n\nBusiness rule\n\n## When to use\n\nA rule the solution must enforce, with explicit constraints. Points at what it governs via appliesTo; also add an appliesTo edge so diagrams can anchor it.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  |  |\n| `appliesTo` | array of object | **yes** |  |  |\n| `appliesTo[].kind` | string | **yes** | `Application` · `BusinessCapability` · `BusinessService` |  |\n| `appliesTo[].name` | string | **yes** |  | metadata.name of the governed manifest |\n| `constraints` | array of object | **yes** |  |  |\n| `constraints[].name` | string | **yes** |  | Constraint identifier |\n| `constraints[].condition` | string | **yes** |  | Business rule condition statement |\n| `constraints[].error` | string | **yes** |  | Error message when constraint is violated |\n| `constraints[].action` | string |  |  | Action to take on violation (optional) |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: BusinessRule\nmetadata:\n  name: example-businessrule\nspec:\n  description: example-description\n  appliesTo:\n    - kind: Application\n      name: example-name\n  constraints:\n    - name: example-name\n      condition: example-condition\n      error: example-error\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/businessservice.md":
    "# BusinessService\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: BusinessService`\n\nBusiness service\n\n## When to use\n\nA service the business delivers to customers or other areas. Annotation, not topology.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  |  |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  | Friendly display name |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: BusinessService\nmetadata:\n  name: example-businessservice\nspec:\n  description: example-description\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/cache.md":
    "# Cache\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Cache`\n\nCache\n\n## When to use\n\nDisposable, fast data in front of something slower (sessions, computed results, rate limits). Losing it costs latency, not data.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `ElastiCache Redis`, `ElastiCache Valkey`, `ElastiCache Memcached`, `MemoryDB`<br>azure: `Azure Cache for Redis`<br>gcp: `Memorystore Redis`, `Memorystore Memcached`<br>self-management: `Redis`, `Valkey`, `Memcached`, `Hazelcast` |  |\n| `description` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Cache\nmetadata:\n  name: example-cache\nspec:\n  provider: ElastiCache Redis\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/channel.md":
    "# Channel\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Channel`\n\nChannel through which people use the solution — mobile app, web app, desktop, chatbot\n\n## When to use\n\nThe front end people interact with. It calls an APIGateway, LoadBalancer or Application; nothing calls it.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `type` | string | **yes** | `Mobile` · `Web` · `Desktop` · `Chatbot` |  |\n| `platform` | string |  | `iOS` · `Android` · `CrossPlatform` · `Browser` | Target platform |\n| `framework` | string |  | e.g. `React`, `Angular`, `Next.js`, `Flutter`, `React Native`, `SwiftUI`, `Jetpack Compose` |  |\n| `description` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Channel\nmetadata:\n  name: example-channel\nspec:\n  type: Mobile\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/community.md":
    "# Community\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Community`\n\nCommunity of teams\n\n## When to use\n\nGroup of squads. Organization metadata — not drawn as part of the architecture; edges are optional.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `code` | string | **yes** |  | Community identifier/code |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  | Friendly display name for the community |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Community\nmetadata:\n  name: example-community\nspec:\n  code: example-code\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/database.md":
    "# Database\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Database`\n\nDatabase\n\n## When to use\n\nSystem of record — data that must survive restarts and is queried (SQL, document, key-value, graph, search). Use Cache when the data can be rebuilt, Storage for files.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `DynamoDB`, `AuroraMySQL`, `RDSMySQL`, `AuroraPostgres`, `RDSPostgres`, `RDSSQLServer`, `RDSOracle`, `Neptune`, `DocumentDB`, `Redshift`, `OpenSearch`<br>azure: `CosmosDB`, `AzureSQL`, `AzurePostgres`, `AzureMySQL`<br>gcp: `CloudSQLPostgres`, `CloudSQLMySQL`, `AlloyDB`, `Spanner`, `Firestore`, `Bigtable`, `BigQuery`<br>onpremise: `Oracle`, `SQLServer`, `Db2`<br>self-management: `PostgreSQL`, `MySQL`, `MariaDB`, `MongoDB`, `Cassandra`, `Elasticsearch`, `Neo4j`, `ClickHouse` | Database product/service |\n| `description` | string |  |  | Database description |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Database\nmetadata:\n  name: example-database\nspec:\n  provider: DynamoDB\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/domain.md":
    "# Domain\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Domain`\n\nDDD domain — the business area the software serves (e.g. Commerce, Finance, Logistics)\n\n## When to use\n\nA business area in the Domain-Driven Design sense. Model it when the user talks about domains or business areas, or when the solution spans more than one. Subdomains belong to it; it belongs to nothing. Not a technical component — it never takes part in runtime flow.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  | What the business does in this area |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  |  |\n| `owner` | string |  |  | Business area or person accountable for the domain |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Domain\nmetadata:\n  name: example-domain\nspec:\n  description: example-description\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/externalsystem.md":
    "# ExternalSystem\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: ExternalSystem`\n\nA system outside the solution that it integrates with — a partner, a SaaS product, another area's platform\n\n## When to use\n\nAnything the solution talks to but does not build or operate — payment acquirer, CRM, ERP, identity provider, partner or government API. Applications call it (or it calls an APIGateway). Do not use it for managed infrastructure that has its own Kind (database, queue, notification service).\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  | What the system is and what the solution uses it for |\n| `category` | string |  | `Payment` · `CRM` · `ERP` · `Identity` · `Partner` · `Government` · `Analytics` · `Other` |  |\n| `vendor` | string |  | e.g. `Stripe`, `Salesforce`, `SAP`, `Auth0` | Product or company, when known |\n| `owner` | string |  |  | Who operates it — a vendor, a partner, another area of the organization |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: ExternalSystem\nmetadata:\n  name: example-externalsystem\nspec:\n  description: example-description\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/loadbalancer.md":
    "# LoadBalancer\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: LoadBalancer`\n\nLoad balancer\n\n## When to use\n\nDistributes traffic across instances of Applications (ALB/NLB, NGINX, HAProxy, F5). Model it only when it matters to the reader; edges go LoadBalancer → Application with type calls.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `ALB`, `NLB`<br>azure: `Azure Load Balancer`, `Azure Application Gateway`, `Azure Front Door`<br>gcp: `Cloud Load Balancing`<br>onpremise: `F5`<br>self-management: `NGINX`, `HAProxy`, `Envoy`, `Traefik` |  |\n| `exposure` | string |  | `Internal` · `External` · `InternalExternal` | Who can reach it — Internal, External or both |\n| `description` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: LoadBalancer\nmetadata:\n  name: example-loadbalancer\nspec:\n  provider: ALB\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/notification.md":
    "# Notification\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Notification`\n\nOutbound notification service — email, SMS, push, chat messages to people\n\n## When to use\n\nA service that delivers messages to people (SES, FCM, Twilio, SendGrid). Applications call it. Not the app the user opens — that is a Channel.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `SES`, `SNS Mobile Push`, `Pinpoint`<br>azure: `Azure Communication Services`, `Azure Notification Hubs`<br>gcp: `Firebase Cloud Messaging`<br>self-management: `SMTP`<br>saas: `APNs`, `SendGrid`, `Mailgun`, `Twilio`, `OneSignal` |  |\n| `type` | string | **yes** | `Email` · `SMS` · `Push` · `Chat` | Delivery medium |\n| `description` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Notification\nmetadata:\n  name: example-notification\nspec:\n  provider: SES\n  type: Email\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/queue.md":
    "# Queue\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Queue`\n\nQueue\n\n## When to use\n\nPoint-to-point async work — each message is handled by one consumer. Use Topic when several independent subscribers receive the same message.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `SQS`, `Amazon MQ`<br>azure: `Azure Service Bus`, `Azure Queue Storage`<br>gcp: `Cloud Tasks`<br>onpremise: `IBM MQ`<br>self-management: `RabbitMQ`, `ActiveMQ`, `NATS` |  |\n| `description` | string |  |  | Queue description |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Queue\nmetadata:\n  name: example-queue\nspec:\n  provider: SQS\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/relationship.md":
    "# Relationship\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Relationship`\n\nRelationship graph: a list of directed edges (from -> to)\n\n## When to use\n\nThe graph. Each edge connects two declared manifests by kind + metadata.name. One Relationship per solution is typical.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `edges` | array of object | **yes** | at least 1 | List of directed edges between components |\n| `edges[].from` | object | **yes** |  | Composite key (kind + id) referencing a workspace component |\n| `edges[].from.kind` | string | **yes** |  | Kind of the referenced component (asserted against the resolved component) |\n| `edges[].from.id` | string | **yes** |  | metadata.name of the referenced component |\n| `edges[].to` | object | **yes** |  | Composite key (kind + id) referencing a workspace component |\n| `edges[].to.kind` | string | **yes** |  | Kind of the referenced component (asserted against the resolved component) |\n| `edges[].to.id` | string | **yes** |  | metadata.name of the referenced component |\n| `edges[].type` | string | **yes** | `calls` · `consumes` · `produces` · `reads` · `writes` · `subscribes` · `publishes` · `triggers` · `appliesTo` · `belongsTo` · `relatedTo` | Type of relationship |\n| `edges[].description` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Relationship\nmetadata:\n  name: example-relationship\nspec:\n  edges:\n    - from:\n        kind: example-kind\n        id: example-id\n      to:\n        kind: example-kind\n        id: example-id\n      type: calls\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/serviceoffer.md":
    "# ServiceOffer\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: ServiceOffer`\n\nService offer\n\n## When to use\n\nA commercial offer built on business services (a product the customer buys). Annotation, not topology.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  |  |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  | Friendly display name |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: ServiceOffer\nmetadata:\n  name: example-serviceoffer\nspec:\n  description: example-description\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/squad.md":
    "# Squad\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Squad`\n\nMultidisciplinary team\n\n## When to use\n\nTeam that owns applications. Organization metadata — not drawn as part of the architecture; edges are optional.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `code` | string | **yes** |  | Squad identifier/code |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  | Friendly display name for the squad |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Squad\nmetadata:\n  name: example-squad\nspec:\n  code: example-code\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/storage.md":
    "# Storage\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Storage`\n\nObject, file or block storage\n\n## When to use\n\nFiles and blobs addressed by key or path — uploads, exports, reports, backups, static assets. Use Database for queried records.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `S3`, `S3 Glacier`, `EFS`, `EBS`, `FSx`<br>azure: `Azure Blob Storage`, `Azure Files`<br>gcp: `Cloud Storage`, `Filestore`<br>onpremise: `NAS`, `SAN`<br>self-management: `MinIO`, `Ceph` |  |\n| `description` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Storage\nmetadata:\n  name: example-storage\nspec:\n  provider: S3\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/subdomain.md":
    "# Subdomain\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Subdomain`\n\nDDD subdomain — a part of a domain's problem space, classified as core, supporting or generic\n\n## When to use\n\nA part of a Domain (Subdomain → Domain, belongsTo). ApplicationServices — the bounded contexts — belong to the subdomain they implement. Classify it with type; it decides where to build versus buy.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `type` | string | **yes** | `core` · `supporting` · `generic` | core = what differentiates the business (build, best people); supporting = needed and specific, not differentiating; generic = solved problem (buy or use off-the-shelf) |\n| `description` | string | **yes** |  |  |\n| `displayName` | string |  |  |  |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Subdomain\nmetadata:\n  name: example-subdomain\nspec:\n  type: core\n  description: example-description\n```\n",
  "references/kinds/topic.md":
    "# Topic\n\n`apiVersion: opscr.dev/v1` · `kind: Topic`\n\nTopic\n\n## When to use\n\nPublish/subscribe — every subscriber receives each message (SNS, Kafka, Pub/Sub, Event Hubs). Classify it with type.\n\n## `spec` fields\n\nOnly these fields are allowed; anything else is a validation error.\n\n| Field | Type | Required | Values | Description |\n|-------|------|----------|--------|-------------|\n| `provider` | string | **yes** | aws: `SNS`, `MSK`, `Kinesis`, `EventBridge`<br>azure: `Azure Event Hubs`, `Azure Event Grid`<br>gcp: `Google Pub/Sub`<br>self-management: `Kafka`, `RabbitMQ`, `NATS`, `Pulsar` |  |\n| `type` | string | **yes** | `Command` · `DomainEvent` · `IntegrationEvent` · `Reply` | Command = a request for one handler to execute; DomainEvent = a fact consumed inside the same solution; IntegrationEvent = a business fact published for the rest of the organization; Reply = the answer to an earlier Command |\n| `description` | string |  |  | Topic description |\n\n## Minimal valid manifest\n\nRequired fields only. Replace the placeholder values.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Topic\nmetadata:\n  name: example-topic\nspec:\n  provider: SNS\n  type: Command\n```\n",
  "references/patterns/ddd.md":
    "# Pattern: Domain-Driven Design (strategic)\n\nRead this when the user talks about domains, subdomains, bounded contexts or business areas, or when the solution has more than one `ApplicationService`. Enable it with `ddd` in `opscr.config.yaml` → `packs`.\n\n## The hierarchy\n\n| DDD concept | opscr | Edge |\n|-------------|-------|------|\n| Domain — a business area (Commerce, Finance) | `Domain` | belongs to nothing |\n| Subdomain — a part of the domain's problem space | `Subdomain` with `type` | `Subdomain -belongsTo-> Domain` |\n| Bounded context — one model, one team, one language | `ApplicationService` | `ApplicationService -belongsTo-> Subdomain` (or `-> Domain` when there are no subdomains) |\n| The software implementing that context | `Application`s | `Application -belongsTo-> ApplicationService` |\n\nDiagrams nest along these edges. Only these combinations compile; anything else under a `Domain` or `Subdomain` is a core error.\n\n`BusinessCapability` is not a subdomain: a capability is what the business is able to do; a subdomain is how you partition the problem to draw software boundaries. Use subdomains for boundaries and capabilities only when the user talks about them.\n\n## Classifying subdomains\n\n- **core** — what makes the business different. Build it, with the best people; it gets the richest model.\n- **supporting** — specific to the business but not differentiating. Build simply, or outsource.\n- **generic** — a solved problem (billing, identity, email). Buy or use a SaaS: often the implementation is an `ExternalSystem`, not your own Application.\n\nIf the user cannot say which is which, the default is: what they describe in most detail is core; payments, identity and notifications are generic.\n\n## Boundaries\n\n- **One bounded context per subdomain** is the default. A context spanning several subdomains mixes models that change for different reasons.\n- **Each context owns its data.** Only its applications write its databases. Two contexts writing the same store share a model through the schema; reading another context's store couples you to its internals.\n- **Contexts integrate through APIs or events**: `calls` to an Application of the other context, or a `Topic` it publishes (see the eda pattern). Prefer events between domains.\n- A **generic** subdomain is often an `ExternalSystem` you call, wrapped by a small context of yours (an anti-corruption layer) when its model leaks.\n\n## Files\n\nWith **two or more `Domain`s**, write one file per Domain, named after it (`commerce.opscr.yaml`), holding the Domain, its Subdomains, its ApplicationServices, their Applications and the data and messaging they own. What several domains use (channels, a shared gateway) goes in `shared.opscr.yaml`; every edge in `relationships.opscr.yaml`.\n\n## Rules checked by `opscr compile --pack ddd`\n\n| Rule | Severity | What it reports |\n|------|----------|-----------------|\n| `ddd/subdomain-without-domain` | warning | a Subdomain that belongs to no Domain floats outside the model |\n| `ddd/context-without-subdomain` | warning | once the solution declares Subdomains, every ApplicationService (bounded context) belongs to the Subdomain it implements |\n| `ddd/context-in-several-subdomains` | warning | a bounded context implementing several subdomains usually mixes models that change for different reasons |\n| `ddd/application-without-context` | warning | once the solution declares ApplicationServices, every Application belongs to one — otherwise nobody owns its model |\n| `ddd/shared-datastore` | warning | a Database, Cache or Storage written by applications of different bounded contexts couples their models through the schema |\n| `ddd/foreign-data-access` | warning | an application reading data written by another bounded context reaches into its model; ask that context (API or events) instead |\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Eric Evans — *Domain-Driven Design* (Part IV, strategic design)\n- Vaughn Vernon — *Domain-Driven Design Distilled*\n- Martin Fowler — \"BoundedContext\" (martinfowler.com/bliki/BoundedContext.html)\n",
  "references/patterns/eda.md":
    '# Pattern: event-driven architecture\n\nRead this when the solution has a `Topic` or a `Queue`, or the user talks about events, commands, async processing, pub/sub or sagas. Then enable the pack so `opscr compile` checks it: put `packs: [eda]` in `opscr.config.yaml` in the solution directory.\n\n## 1. Pick the channel\n\n| You need | Use | Edges |\n|----------|-----|-------|\n| Work done once, by one worker (jobs, payments to charge, emails to send) | `Queue` | producer `produces` → Queue; Queue `triggers` → worker, or worker `consumes` → Queue |\n| Every interested party told that something happened | `Topic` with an event `type` | publisher `publishes` → Topic; each subscriber `subscribes` → Topic, or Topic `triggers` → subscriber |\n| One specific component asked to do something, asynchronously | `Topic` with `type: Command` | sender `publishes` → Topic; exactly one handler receives it |\n| The answer to an earlier command | `Topic` with `type: Reply` | handler `publishes` → Topic; the original sender receives it |\n\n`Topic.type`:\n\n- **`DomainEvent`** — a fact inside the solution ("order placed"), consumed by applications of the same solution.\n- **`IntegrationEvent`** — a business fact published for the rest of the organization. Its consumers usually live outside this solution, so it may have none here.\n- **`Command`** — "please do X", addressed to one handler.\n- **`Reply`** — the answer to a `Command`.\n\nIf two different components each need every message, that is a Topic, not a Queue: a Queue hands each message to only one of them.\n\n## 2. Ownership\n\n- **One publisher per event Topic.** An event states a fact about its publisher\'s own state. Name the Topic after the fact, in the past tense: `order-placed`, `payment-failed`. Several publishers on one Topic usually means nobody owns it.\n- **One handler per command.** Name commands in the imperative: `charge-payment`, `ship-order`; their replies after the command: `charge-payment-result`.\n- Prefer several focused Topics over one Topic carrying every event of a domain.\n\n## 3. Coordinating a multi-step flow (saga)\n\n- **Choreography** — each application reacts to the previous one\'s events and publishes its own. Few steps, no central owner needed. Model: chains of `publishes` → Topic → `triggers`/`subscribes`.\n- **Orchestration** — one Application drives the steps: it publishes `Command` Topics and listens to their `Reply` Topics. Prefer it when there are many steps, compensations (undo on failure) or a timeout to manage. Model the orchestrator as an Application and say "orchestrator of <flow>" in its `description`.\n\nWrite compensations ("refund if shipping fails") in the descriptions of the applications that perform them.\n\n## 4. Reliability\n\n- **Dead-letter queue.** For every consumed Queue, declare a second Queue for messages that keep failing, annotate the source and draw the edge:\n\n  ```yaml\n  kind: Queue\n  metadata:\n    name: payment-requests\n    annotations:\n      eda.opscr.dev/dead-letter-queue: payment-requests-dlq\n  ---\n  # in the Relationship\n  - from: { kind: Queue, id: payment-requests }\n    to: { kind: Queue, id: payment-requests-dlq }\n    type: produces\n    description: "Dead letters after retries"\n  ```\n\n- **Dead letters for Topic subscribers.** A subscriber\'s failures need a home too. Declare the Queue, annotate the **subscribing Application**, and draw the edge from it:\n\n  ```yaml\n  kind: Application\n  metadata:\n    name: crm-sync\n    annotations:\n      eda.opscr.dev/dead-letter-queue: crm-sync-dlq\n  ---\n  - from: { kind: Application, id: crm-sync }\n    to: { kind: Queue, id: crm-sync-dlq }\n    type: produces\n    description: "Events it failed to process"\n  ```\n\n- **Idempotent consumers.** Messages can be delivered more than once. Say how the consumer deduplicates (idempotency key, upsert) in its `description`.\n- **Publish what you stored.** An application that writes its database and then publishes can lose the event if it crashes in between. Either use a transactional outbox (say so in the application\'s `description`) or publish from the database\'s change stream: `Database` `triggers` → Application `publishes` → Topic.\n\n## 5. Sync or async\n\n- Use `calls` when the caller needs the answer to continue (queries, validations shown to a user).\n- Use a Topic or Queue when the caller only needs the work to happen eventually, and between different `ApplicationService`s whenever possible, so one being down does not take the other with it.\n- A chain of synchronous `calls` across several applications is as available as its weakest link.\n\n## Rules checked by `opscr compile --pack eda`\n\n| Rule | Severity | What it reports |\n|------|----------|-----------------|\n| `eda/messaging-edge-type` | warning | `publishes`/`subscribes` target a Topic and `produces`/`consumes` target a Queue; mixing them hides which semantics (fan-out or work queue) the design relies on. |\n| `eda/topic-without-publisher` | warning | A Topic nobody publishes to is either dead or missing its producer. |\n| `eda/topic-without-subscriber` | warning | A Command, DomainEvent or Reply Topic with no receiver in the solution. IntegrationEvent is exempt: its subscribers live outside. |\n| `eda/event-multiple-publishers` | warning | An event states a fact about its owner\'s state, so one Topic of events has one publisher. Several publishers usually means a shared, ownerless Topic. |\n| `eda/command-multiple-handlers` | warning | A command asks one handler to do something; several receivers of a Command Topic will each execute it. |\n| `eda/queue-without-producer` | warning | A Queue nobody writes to (dead-letter queues are exempt). |\n| `eda/queue-without-consumer` | warning | A Queue nobody reads from — messages pile up (dead-letter queues are exempt). |\n| `eda/queue-multiple-consumers` | warning | Different components consuming one Queue split its messages between them; if each needs every message, use a Topic. |\n| `eda/dead-letter-missing` | warning | The `eda.opscr.dev/dead-letter-queue` annotation names a Queue that is not declared. |\n| `eda/subscriber-without-dead-letter` | info | An Application receiving from a Topic without `eda.opscr.dev/dead-letter-queue`: a message its subscription keeps failing on is lost. |\n| `eda/queue-without-dead-letter` | info | A consumed Queue without `eda.opscr.dev/dead-letter-queue`: say where messages that keep failing go. |\n\n## Further reading\n\n- Gregor Hohpe, Bobby Woolf — *Enterprise Integration Patterns* (enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com)\n- Martin Fowler — "What do you mean by Event-Driven?" (martinfowler.com/articles/201701-event-driven.html)\n- Chris Richardson — Saga and Transactional Outbox patterns (microservices.io/patterns/data/saga.html, microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html)\n',
  "references/patterns/topology.md":
    "# Pattern: topology\n\nRead this before drawing edges whenever the solution has gateways, load balancers, channels, database triggers or more than one cloud. Enable it with `packs: [topology]` in `opscr.config.yaml` (alongside other packs, e.g. `[eda, topology]`).\n\nThe core rules in `references/relationships.md` reject connections that cannot exist. This pack covers connections that **can** exist but are usually a design mistake — and the ones whose validity depends on the `provider`.\n\n## Front doors\n\n- A `Channel` reaches the backend through **one** front door: an `APIGateway` (or a `LoadBalancer` when there is no gateway). If the solution has a gateway, no Channel should call an Application around it.\n- Gateways and load balancers forward to **Applications**. Data, queues and topics sit behind an Application that owns the logic.\n- Exceptions are real and provider-specific: an AWS API Gateway can integrate straight with DynamoDB, SQS, SNS, Kinesis, EventBridge and S3; a browser or app can upload to S3 / Cloud Storage / Blob Storage with presigned or SAS URLs; a Firestore client SDK talks to the database under security rules. These compile as `info`: say in the description why the shortcut is acceptable.\n\n## Database triggers\n\n`Database -triggers-> Application` only works when the database has a change stream: DynamoDB, CosmosDB, Firestore, MongoDB, DocumentDB, Spanner, Neptune. For any other database, model the change-data-capture connector (Debezium, DMS…) as an Application that `reads` the database and publishes what changed.\n\n## More than one cloud\n\nEdges between aws, azure and gcp components are reported as `info`: they cost latency, egress and a second identity system. If the split is intended, write why in the edge's `description` — it is what a diagram reader will ask first.\n\n## Turning rules down\n\nA project can change the severity of any rule of this pack:\n\n```yaml\n# opscr.config.yaml\npacks: [topology]\nrules:\n  topology/cross-cloud-edge: off\n  topology/channel-bypasses-gateway: info\n```\n\nCore rules cannot be turned down.\n\n## Rules checked by `opscr compile --pack topology`\n\n| Rule | Connection | Severity | Why | Exceptions |\n|------|------------|----------|-----|------------|\n| `topology/gateway-to-datastore` | APIGateway —any→ Database / Cache / Storage / Topic / Queue | error | an API gateway reaching data or messaging directly, with no Application in between | AWS API Gateway → DynamoDB, SQS, SNS, Kinesis, EventBridge, S3: info — native service integration; works, but request mapping and validation now live in the gateway |\n| `topology/load-balancer-target` | LoadBalancer —any→ Database / Cache / Storage / Topic / Queue / Notification | error | a load balancer spreads traffic across instances of a service; it does not front data, messaging or notification services | — |\n| `topology/channel-to-backend-store` | Channel —any→ Database / Cache / Storage / Topic / Queue | error | a front end talking to data or messaging directly, without a backend in between | any → Firestore: info — client SDK guarded by security rules — say so in the Channel's description; any → Storage S3, Cloud Storage, Azure Blob Storage (reads/writes): info — direct upload/download with presigned or SAS URLs issued by a backend |\n| `topology/database-trigger-without-stream` | Database —triggers→ any | warning | a database triggering code needs a change stream; DynamoDB, CosmosDB, Firestore, MongoDB, DocumentDB, Spanner, Neptune have one natively | DynamoDB, CosmosDB, Firestore, MongoDB, DocumentDB, Spanner, Neptune → any: allowed |\n\n| Rule | Severity | What it reports |\n|------|----------|-----------------|\n| `topology/channel-bypasses-gateway` | warning | a Channel calling an Application or LoadBalancer directly while the solution has an APIGateway — one front door, not two |\n| `topology/cross-cloud-edge` | info | an edge between components on different clouds (aws, azure, gcp): latency, egress cost and two identity systems to wire |\n",
  "references/providers.md":
    "# Providers\n\n`spec.provider` is a **string**: the concrete product or managed service, not just the cloud.\nPick the value from the row of the Kind you are writing; the column says which platform it implies.\n\n```yaml\nkind: Database\nspec:\n  provider: RDSPostgres   # not `aws`, not `PostgreSQL on AWS`\n```\n\n- `onpremise`: vendor products run in your own data center.\n- `self-management`: open-source software you operate yourself (VMs, Kubernetes, containers), on any infrastructure.\n- `saas`: third-party services you only consume through their API.\n- Keep one platform per solution unless the user says otherwise.\n\n| Kind | aws | azure | gcp | onpremise | self-management | saas |\n|------|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `APIGateway` | `AWS API Gateway` | `Azure API Management` | `Google API Gateway`, `Apigee` |  | `Kong`, `Tyk`, `KrakenD` |  |\n| `LoadBalancer` | `ALB`, `NLB` | `Azure Load Balancer`, `Azure Application Gateway`, `Azure Front Door` | `Cloud Load Balancing` | `F5` | `NGINX`, `HAProxy`, `Envoy`, `Traefik` |  |\n| `Application` | `ECS`, `EKS`, `Lambda`, `Fargate`, `EC2`, `App Runner` | `AKS`, `Azure Functions`, `Azure App Service`, `Azure Container Apps` | `GKE`, `Cloud Run`, `Cloud Functions`, `App Engine` | `OpenShift`, `VM` | `Kubernetes`, `Docker` |  |\n| `Topic` | `SNS`, `MSK`, `Kinesis`, `EventBridge` | `Azure Event Hubs`, `Azure Event Grid` | `Google Pub/Sub` |  | `Kafka`, `RabbitMQ`, `NATS`, `Pulsar` |  |\n| `Queue` | `SQS`, `Amazon MQ` | `Azure Service Bus`, `Azure Queue Storage` | `Cloud Tasks` | `IBM MQ` | `RabbitMQ`, `ActiveMQ`, `NATS` |  |\n| `Database` | `DynamoDB`, `AuroraMySQL`, `RDSMySQL`, `AuroraPostgres`, `RDSPostgres`, `RDSSQLServer`, `RDSOracle`, `Neptune`, `DocumentDB`, `Redshift`, `OpenSearch` | `CosmosDB`, `AzureSQL`, `AzurePostgres`, `AzureMySQL` | `CloudSQLPostgres`, `CloudSQLMySQL`, `AlloyDB`, `Spanner`, `Firestore`, `Bigtable`, `BigQuery` | `Oracle`, `SQLServer`, `Db2` | `PostgreSQL`, `MySQL`, `MariaDB`, `MongoDB`, `Cassandra`, `Elasticsearch`, `Neo4j`, `ClickHouse` |  |\n| `Cache` | `ElastiCache Redis`, `ElastiCache Valkey`, `ElastiCache Memcached`, `MemoryDB` | `Azure Cache for Redis` | `Memorystore Redis`, `Memorystore Memcached` |  | `Redis`, `Valkey`, `Memcached`, `Hazelcast` |  |\n| `Storage` | `S3`, `S3 Glacier`, `EFS`, `EBS`, `FSx` | `Azure Blob Storage`, `Azure Files` | `Cloud Storage`, `Filestore` | `NAS`, `SAN` | `MinIO`, `Ceph` |  |\n| `Notification` | `SES`, `SNS Mobile Push`, `Pinpoint` | `Azure Communication Services`, `Azure Notification Hubs` | `Firebase Cloud Messaging` |  | `SMTP` | `APNs`, `SendGrid`, `Mailgun`, `Twilio`, `OneSignal` |\n",
  "references/relationships.md":
    '# Relationships\n\nEdges live in `kind: Relationship` manifests under `spec.edges[]`. An edge is directed: `from` acts on `to`.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: opscr.dev/v1\nkind: Relationship\nmetadata:\n  name: <solution>-relationships\nspec:\n  edges:\n    - from:\n        kind: Application\n        id: orders-api\n      to:\n        kind: Database\n        id: orders-db\n      type: writes\n      description: "Persists orders"   # optional, short, says *what* flows\n```\n\n`kind` + `id` must match a declared manifest\'s `kind` and `metadata.name` exactly.\n\n## Edge types\n\nAllowed values: `calls`, `consumes`, `produces`, `reads`, `writes`, `subscribes`, `publishes`, `triggers`, `appliesTo`, `belongsTo`, `relatedTo`.\n\n| Type | Meaning (`from` → `to`) | Typical pair |\n|------|-------------------------|--------------|\n| `calls` | synchronous request/response, including traffic forwarded by a gateway or load balancer | Channel / APIGateway / LoadBalancer / Application → APIGateway / LoadBalancer / Application / Notification / ExternalSystem; ExternalSystem → APIGateway (a partner calling in) |\n| `reads` | reads data | Application → Database / Cache / Storage |\n| `writes` | writes data | Application → Database / Cache / Storage |\n| `publishes` | emits a message to a topic | Application → Topic |\n| `subscribes` | receives messages from a topic | Application → Topic |\n| `produces` | enqueues work | Application → Queue |\n| `consumes` | takes work from a queue | Application → Queue |\n| `triggers` | the platform invokes `to` | Queue / Topic / Database (stream) / Storage (event) → Application |\n| `belongsTo` | containment, not flow | Application → ApplicationService; ApplicationService → Subdomain (or Domain); Subdomain → Domain |\n| `appliesTo` | annotation, not flow | BusinessRule / BusinessCapability / BusinessService → Application / ApplicationService |\n| `relatedTo` | weak dependency; use only when nothing above fits | any → any |\n\n## Connections that never compile\n\nThese are errors in every project — the graph they describe cannot exist. An edge from a component to itself is also rejected.\n\n| Rule | Connection | Why | Exceptions |\n|------|------------|-----|------------|\n| `core/non-runtime-flow` | Domain / Subdomain / BusinessCapability / BusinessService / ServiceOffer / BusinessRule / Squad / Community / ApplicationService —calls, reads, writes, publishes, subscribes, produces, consumes, triggers→ any | business, organization and ApplicationService manifests do not take part in runtime flow; they only use belongsTo, appliesTo or relatedTo | — |\n| `core/passive-caller` | Database / Cache / Storage / Topic / Queue / Notification —calls→ any | databases, caches, storage, topics, queues and notification services do not call anything | — |\n| `core/into-channel` | any —calls, reads, writes, publishes, subscribes, produces, consumes, triggers→ Channel | a Channel is where people enter the solution; nothing calls, writes to or triggers it | — |\n| `core/data-verb-target` | any —reads, writes→ not Database / Cache / Storage | reads and writes target a Database, Cache or Storage | — |\n| `core/messaging-verb-target` | any —publishes, subscribes, produces, consumes→ not Topic / Queue | publishes, subscribes, produces and consumes target a Topic or a Queue | — |\n| `core/trigger-source` | Channel / APIGateway / LoadBalancer / Cache / Notification —triggers→ any | triggers means the platform invokes the target on a message, a data change or an external event; gateways, load balancers, channels, caches and notification services emit none of those | — |\n| `core/domain-is-root` | Domain —belongsTo→ any | a Domain is the top of the DDD hierarchy; it belongs to nothing | — |\n| `core/subdomain-parent` | Subdomain —belongsTo→ not Domain | a Subdomain belongs to a Domain | — |\n| `core/domain-members` | any —belongsTo→ Domain / Subdomain | only Subdomains and ApplicationServices (bounded contexts) belong to a Domain; only ApplicationServices belong to a Subdomain | Subdomain / ApplicationService → Domain: allowed; ApplicationService → Subdomain: allowed |\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Point the edge from the actor to the thing it acts on: the Application `writes` the Database, not the reverse.\n- Queues: the producer `produces` → Queue; the consumer `consumes` → Queue. Use `triggers` (Queue → Application) instead of `consumes` only when the platform invokes the consumer (SQS → Lambda).\n- Topics: the publisher `publishes` → Topic; each subscriber `subscribes` → Topic, or the Topic `triggers` the subscriber when the platform pushes to it.\n- `belongsTo` and `appliesTo` describe structure, not runtime flow. Diagrams draw `belongsTo` as nesting and `appliesTo` as an attached note.\n- Prefer the specific type. `relatedTo` carries no meaning for a reader or a diagram.\n- Do not add an edge back just to show a response; `calls` already implies it. Reverse edges create cycle warnings.\n- A read and a write between the same pair are two edges only if both matter; otherwise keep `writes`.\n',
};
